{:edb/db-before-t 1559 :edb/db-after-t 1560 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534872 50 #inst "2026-10-12T22:19:34.237Z" 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186144854 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144857 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186144980 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144985 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186144992 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144997 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147248 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of flowers in the garden, which is $40$ flowers. The percentage is $30 \\%,$ and the part is the number of roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = 30 \\div 100 = 0.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =30%\\times 40 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 40 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = \\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =30%\\times 40 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\times 40 \\\\  & =3\\times \\frac{40}{10} \\\\  & =3\\times 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden." 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147248 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of flowers in the garden, which is $40$ flowers. The percentage is $30 \\%$, and the part is the number of roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = 30 \\div 100 = 0.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 30% \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 0.3 \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = \\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 30% \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10} \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{40}{10} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden." 13194139534872 17592186045417 false] [17592186147249 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of hours Dora works yearly, which is $800$ hours. The percentage is $12.5 \\%,$ and the part is the number of hours she rests.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = 12.5 \\div 100 = 0.125\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.5%\\times 800 \\\\  & =0.125\\times 800 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = \\dfrac{12.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12.5%\\times 800 \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5}{100}\\times 800 \\\\  & =12.5\\times \\frac{800}{100} \\\\  & =12.5\\times 8 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours." 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147249 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of hours Dora works yearly, which is $800$ hours. The percentage is $12.5 \\%$, and the part is the number of hours she rests.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = 12.5 \\div 100 = 0.125\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 12.5% \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 0.125 \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = \\dfrac{12.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 12.5% \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= \\frac{12.5}{100} \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 12.5 \\cdot \\frac{800}{100} \\\\\n&= 12.5 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours." 13194139534872 17592186045417 false] [17592186147250 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}=  \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first tank, which is $1,500$ liters. The percentage is $130 \\%,$ and the part is the capacity of the second tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = 130 \\div 100 = 1.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =130%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =1.3\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =1,950.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = \\dfrac{130}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =130%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{130}{100}\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =130\\times \\frac{1,500}{100} \\\\  & =130\\times 15 \\\\  & =1,950.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters." 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147250 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first tank, which is $1,500$ liters. The percentage is $130 \\%$, and the part is the capacity of the second tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = 130 \\div 100 = 1.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 130% \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 1.3 \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 1, 950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = \\dfrac{130}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 130% \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= \\frac{130}{100} \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 130 \\cdot \\frac{1, 500}{100} \\\\\n&= 130 \\cdot 15 \\\\\n&= 1, 950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters." 13194139534872 17592186045417 false] [17592186147593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442098 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442100 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442102 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442103 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442095 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442096 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442099 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442104 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442097 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442101 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442105 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442106 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147655 1050 420.0 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147850 1071 "The original price of a toy robot in a department store was $$75$. The store manager decides to reduce the price by $12%$. How much of a discount has the manager given?" 13194139534872 17592186045417 false] [17592186147850 1071 "The original price of a toy robot in a department store was $$75.$ The store manager decides to reduce the price by $12%.$ How much of a discount has the manager given?" 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147850 1074 "To find the discount, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the old price, which is $$75.$ The percentage is $12%,$ and the part is the discount in dollars.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12%=12÷100=0.12\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12%\\times 75 \\\\  & =0.12\\times 75 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12%$ of $75$ is $9.$ So, the discount is $$9.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12%=\\frac{12}{100}=\\frac{12÷4}{100÷4}=\\frac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =12%\\times 75 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\times 75 \\\\  & =3\\times \\frac{75}{25} \\\\  & =3\\times 3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12%$ of $75$ is $9.$ So, the discount is $$9.$" 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147871 1071 "Margaret has $$1, 500$ saved. Of this amount, she decides to spend $22.4%$ to buy some supplies for her farm. How much money does Margret plan to spend?" 13194139534872 17592186045417 false] [17592186147871 1071 "Margaret has $$1,500$ saved. Of this amount, she decides to spend $22.4%$ to buy some supplies for her farm. How much money does Margret plan to spend?" 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147871 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of money Margaret has saved, which is $$1,500.$ The percentage is $22.4%,$ and the part is the amount of money that she decides to spend.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n22.4%=22.4÷100=0.224\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =22.4%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =0.224\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =336\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22.4%$ of $1,500$ is $336.$ Thus, Margaret plans to spend $$336.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n22.4%=\\frac{22.4}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =22.4%\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =\\frac{22.4}{100}\\times 1,500 \\\\  & =22.4\\times \\frac{1,500}{100} \\\\  & =22.4\\times 15 \\\\  & =336\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22.4%$ of $1,500$ is $336.$ Thus, Margaret plans to spend $$336.$" 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186147873 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of goals Fernando scored in the first season, which is $8$ goals. The percentage is $250%,$ and the part is the number of goals Fernando scored in the second season.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250%=250÷100=2.5\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =250%\\times 8 \\\\  & =2.5\\times 8 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $250%$ of $8$ is $20.$ So, Fernando scored $20$ goals in his second season.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n250%=\\frac{250}{100}=\\frac{250÷50}{100÷50}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =250%\\times 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\times 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $250%$ of $8$ is $20.$ So, Fernando scored $20$ goals in his second season." 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186148699 1064 "Percentages are *very* common in everyday life. For example, they are used in retail, finance, and sports! In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the percentage of a whole number in real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniel and Luisa traveled $200$ kilometers in their car. If Luisa drove $40 \\%$ of the way, how many kilometers did she drive?\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the whole is the number of kilometers traveled, which is $200$ kilometers. The percentage is $40 \\%$, and the part is the distance Luisa drove.\n\nAs usual, we can use two methods to solve this problem:\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n40 \\% = 40 \\div 100 = 0.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 40% \\cdot 200 \\\\\n&= 0.4 \\cdot 200 \\\\\n&= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo Luisa drove $80$ kilometers.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n40 \\% = \\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 20}{100 \\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 40% \\cdot 200 \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot 200 \\\\\n&= \\frac{400}{5} \\\\\n&= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we find that Luisa drove $80$ kilometers." 13194139534872 17592186045417 false] [17592186148699 1064 "Percentages are *very* common in everyday life. For example, they are used in retail, finance, and sports! In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate the percentage of a whole number in real-world situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Daniel and Luisa traveled $200$ kilometers in their car. If Luisa drove $40 \\%$ of the way, how many kilometers did she drive?\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the whole is the number of kilometers traveled, which is $200$ kilometers. The percentage is $40 \\%,$ and the part is the distance Luisa drove.\n\nAs usual, we can use two methods to solve this problem:\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal: \n\n$$\n40 \\% = 40 \\div 100  = 0.4\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 200 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo Luisa drove $80$ kilometers.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n40 \\% = \\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 20}{100 \\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =40%\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\times 200 \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{5} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we find that Luisa drove $80$ kilometers." 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186442023 1080 "$20$ children" 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186442023 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534872 17592186045417 true] [17592186442023 1083 #uuid "04be5953-70b